    "name":  "Toxic liver disease with chronic active hepatitis without ascites",
    "module":  "hepatobiliary",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Drug-induced liver injury (DILI) causing chronic active hepatitis pattern is a form of hepatotoxicity in which a drug or chemical triggers a persistent autoimmune-like hepatic inflammatory response that continues after the offending agent has been removed. The pattern mimics autoimmune hepatitis histologically with interface hepatitis, plasma cell infiltration, and rosette formation. Common causative agents include nitrofurantoin, minocycline, diclofenac, methyldopa, and herbal supplements. Diagnosis requires temporal association with the drug, exclusion of other causes, and may require liver biopsy for confirmation.",
